Delphi 7 is an older version of Delphi, but I can still help you with rotating a PDF file using Delphi 7.

You'll need to use a third-party component or library to rotate PDF files in Delphi 7. One popular option is the TPDFComponent by Miguel de la Cruz. You can download the component from CodeCentral.

Here's an example of how to use the TPDFComponent to rotate a PDF file:
```delphi
uses
  PDFComponent, pdflib;

var
  PDF: TPDFComponent;
  File: string;

begin
  File := 'path_to_your_pdf_file.pdf';
  PDF := TPDFComponent.Create(nil);
  try
    PDF.LoadFromFile(File);
    PDF.Rotate := pdflib.rpmRotate90; // or rpmRotate180, rpmRotate270
    PDF.SaveToFile('rotated_' + File);
  finally
    PDF.Free;
  end;
end.
```
Make sure to add the TPDFComponent unit to your uses clause and link the pdflib library to your project.

Another option is to use the iText library, which is a popular PDF manipulation library. You can use the iText library in Delphi 7 by adding the iText.dll to your project's directory and using the iText unit.

Here's an example of how to use iText to rotate a PDF file:
```delphi
uses
  iText, iTextSharp;

var
  Reader: TPDFReader;
  Writer: TPDFWriter;
  File: string;

begin
  File := 'path_to_your_pdf_file.pdf';
  Reader := TPDFReader.Create;
  try
    Reader.LoadFromFile(File);
    Writer := TPDFWriter.Create;
    try
      Writer.Rotate(Reader, pdflib.rpmRotate90); // or rpmRotate180, rpmRotate270
      Writer.SaveToFile('rotated_' + File);
    finally
      Writer.Free;
    end;
  finally
    Reader.Free;
  end;
end.
```
Remember to add the iTextSharp unit to your uses clause and link the iTextSharp library to your project.

